      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5acad9ecc9dba010c9c3ec68|full_name}: A compromise could be to implement a system similar to what is used in browsers to determine if punycode (non-ascii codepoint representations in DNS) should be displayed as the original value or the encoded one.  Off the top of my head I believe the metric used by most browsers is to check whether a domain name uses characters from multiple language sets, and also contains ambiguous unicode characters, and if so does not render the unicode encoding.  A similar system could prevent malicious impersonation while also allowing for the use of non-latin characters, since most impersonation requires mixing character sets and using ambiguous characters.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
